@namespace AuthWithAdmin.Client.Components

@*
    MSummaryTag — the page-level "what is currently in here" metadata.

    WHY IT EXISTS
    ─────────────
    The finished Admin headers ended up with two unrelated ways of printing the
    same kind of fact:

      • a loose sentence beside the h1 — `117 פרויקטים · 6 לא מוקצים` on Catalog,
        `N משתמשים · M פעילים` on Users. Plain text with a middle dot, which
        reads as a second subtitle now that the real subtitles are gone;
      • a ROW OF SEPARATE PILLS — three bordered chips on Request Types,
        Milestones and Role Settings. Three containers for three facts about
        one page, each one loud enough to be mistaken for a filter.

    Both are the same object: a small set of related numbers describing what the
    page currently holds. So they are now one object — ONE container, the
    metrics inside it, a hairline between them.

    NOT A CONTROL
    ─────────────
    This is metadata, not a chip, a filter or a status. It has no hover state,
    no pointer cursor, no focus ring and no click handler, and it never carries
    the brand colour. Its whole job is to be readable and then get out of the
    way of the h1.

    THE DIVIDER IS CSS, NOT MARKUP
    ──────────────────────────────
    Drawn by `.m-sumtag-item + .m-sumtag-item::before`, so the caller passes a
    flat list of metrics and never has to interleave separators — and a
    screen reader is not read a column of decorative pipes.

    EMPHASIS, NOT "NUMBER"
    ──────────────────────
    `Value` is the emphasised half and `Label` the quiet one; that is usually a
    count and a noun (`117` · `פרויקטים`), but Cycles uses it for
    `נוכחי` · `2025-2026`, and a page with nothing to count can pass a Value
    alone (`אין עדיין טפסים`). The component does not care which.
*@

@if (Metrics is { Count: > 0 })
{
    <span class="@RootClass">
        @foreach (var m in Metrics)
        {
            var hasValue = !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(m.Value);
            var hasLabel = !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(m.Label);

            <span class="m-sumtag-item">
                @if (hasValue)
                {
                    <span class="m-sumtag-value">@m.Value</span>
                }
                @* A REAL character, not a flex gap. The gap alone separated the
                   two spans visually but put nothing between them in the DOM,
                   so a screen reader read "117פרויקטים" as one word.

                   It has to be `&#160;` and not a plain space: Razor TRIMS a
                   whitespace-only literal, so `<text> </text>` compiled away to
                   nothing and the DOM came back with the two spans still
                   adjacent. A non-breaking space survives the trim, and it also
                   guarantees the count never wraps away from its noun.

                   The item's gap is 0 because this character now carries the
                   separation. *@
                @if (hasValue && hasLabel)
                {
                    <text>&#160;</text>
                }
                @if (hasLabel)
                {
                    <span class="m-sumtag-label">@m.Label</span>
                }
            </span>
        }
    </span>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>One fact about the page. <paramref name="Value"/> is the
    /// emphasised half (usually the count), <paramref name="Label"/> the quiet
    /// one (usually the noun). Either may be empty.</summary>
    public sealed record Metric(string Value, string? Label = null);

    /// <summary>The metrics, in reading order. An empty or null list renders
    /// nothing at all — callers that only have data some of the time can pass
    /// the list unconditionally instead of wrapping the tag in an @@if.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public IReadOnlyList<Metric>? Metrics { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public string? Class { get; set; }

    private string RootClass =>
        "m-sumtag" + (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Class) ? "" : $" {Class}");
}
